# 180 litres in, 1.5 out
subtitle: "Why your kidneys filter 180 litres a day only to take nearly all of it back, and how a percent of that sets your water balance."

## 180 litres in, 1.5 out
use: nephron
with:
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 120
      w: 940
      h: 575
    show_gradient: true
    labels: false

    text n180 "180 litres a day" #b5482e x 150 y 330 font_size 34 hidden true
    text n15 "1.5 litres" #b5482e x 880 y 215 font_size 34 hidden true
    math ctrl tex "99\\% \\text{ kept} \\to 1.8 \\text{ L}" size 30 x 180 y 230 hidden true
    text L_glom "Glomerulus" #5a534c x 175 y 660 font_size 26 hidden true
    text L_prox "Proximal" #5a534c x 425 y 645 font_size 26 hidden true
    text L_dist "Distal" #5a534c x 636 y 640 font_size 26 hidden true
    text L_henle "Loop of Henle" #5a534c x 517 y 150 font_size 26 hidden true
    text L_coll "Collecting duct" #5a534c x 835 y 420 font_size 26 hidden true
    text L_urine "Urine" #5a534c x 825 y 160 font_size 26 hidden true
    text salty "saltier ↓" #b5482e x 365 y 250 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "180 litres in, 1.5 out"

How much urine do your kidneys make? About one and a half litres a day, but they start from far more. It begins in a glomerulus.
    draw afferent glomerulus capsule L_glom

Blood pressure pushes plasma through this knot of capillaries.
    draw efferent

Cells and proteins stay in the blood; water, salt, sugar and waste fall into the capsule.
    highlight capsule

Your two million or so nephrons filter about a hundred and eighty litres a day: all your plasma, some sixty times over.
    draw n180

So the rest of the tubule is about taking it back. The proximal tubule reclaims about two thirds of the water and salt at once.
    draw proximal L_prox arrow_1 arrow_2

And normally all the glucose.
    draw arrow_0

But when blood sugar runs too high, this step is swamped and sugar spills into the urine: that is why it is called diabetes mellitus: mellitus means honey-sweet.
    highlight arrow_0

Then the loop of Henle dives into the medulla, where the tissue grows saltier the deeper it goes.
    draw gradient descending ascending L_henle salty

Going down, water seeps out into that salt. Coming up, salt is pumped out but water can't follow — that is what keeps the deep tissue salty.
    draw arrow_3 arrow_4 parallel true

The distal tubule trims the sodium, on orders from the hormone aldosterone.
    draw distal L_dist arrow_5

The collecting duct runs back down past the salty zone. Antidiuretic hormone opens water channels here, and the salt pulls water out.
    draw collecting L_coll arrow_6

What's left is urine: about one and a half litres of the hundred and eighty. Over ninety-nine percent came back.
    draw urine_arrow L_urine n15

Why filter so much only to take it back? Because anything the tubule doesn't choose to reclaim — waste, toxins, drugs — leaves by default.
    focus glomerulus capsule

    erase n15

It also makes control fine. Keep ninety-nine percent of 180 litres, and you pass 1.8.
    draw ctrl

Keep ninety-eight, and it doubles to 3.6.
    morph ctrl tex "98\\% \\text{ kept} \\to 3.6 \\text{ L}"

So your water balance is set here, by a percent or two of reabsorption, not by filtering less.
    point at.ref arrow_6 gesture circle

    quiz "You drink two extra litres of water. What mainly changes?"
        right: The collecting duct keeps less water: filtration stays near 180 litres, and with less antidiuretic hormone more of it is let go as urine.
        * The glomerulus filters more
        + The collecting duct keeps less water
        * The proximal tubule stops taking back glucose
---
